                   C H A P T E R T W E N T Y- FO U R


                             Morning
                T R AC K   “La Vida Es un Carnaval” — Celia Cruz
                      ECHO      the Armory dance hall, 2057




I woke up on one side of the world.
   The right side had gone out like a TV nobody paid for. Not a
ringing, not a hum. A closed door with a very large room behind it. On
the left, everything: a cart wheel complaining down the hall, a monitor,
a nurse in sneakers asking whether I wanted ice chips, somebody’s
cousin explaining to somebody else’s cousin what the Yankees should
have done in the eighth.
   A doctor came with a clipboard and a gentle voice and said sudden
sensorineural loss, and some may return, and most commonly does not, and I
nodded the way you nod at a weather report for a city you’ve already
decided to leave.
   Mamá was in the chair on my left. She’d moved it there herself, be‐
fore anybody told her.
   “Dejé la radio prendida,” she said.
   “What?”
   “The radio. In the kitchen. When they called. Merengue. All the way
up. I didn’t turn it off all night.”
   “I’m going to be angry at you for a year,” said my mother. “In the
car. In the kitchen. At church. I’m going to be angry in front of the
priest.” She picked up her purse, set it down, picked it up again. “And I
am going to be at every one of your gigs.”



